        [Turn 22 REASONING] (3868 chars)
        \[
\text{Find the smallest }n\ge 866\text{ for which a bijection }f:\{1,\dots ,n\}\to\{1,\dots ,n\}
\]

\[
\text{satisfies } 
\bigl(n\mid 866x-y\bigr)\Longrightarrow\bigl(n+1\mid f(x)^{866}-f(y)\bigr)
\qquad(\forall x,y\in\{1,\dots ,n\}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Re‑writing the condition  

For \(x\in\{1,\dots ,n\}\) put  

\[
\sigma (x)=\bigl((866x-1)\bmod n\bigr)+1 .
\]

Then  

\[
n\mid 866x-y\iff y=\sigma (x).
\]

Hence the required property is  

\[
\boxed{f(\sigma (x))\equiv f(x)^{866}\pmod{n+1}\qquad(\forall x)}\tag{1}
\]

The map \(\sigma\) is the linear map \(x\mapsto 866x\) on the cyclic
additive group \(\mathbb Z_n\) (the element \(n\) represents \(0\)).
The map  

\[
\tau (a)=a^{866}\pmod{n+1}\qquad (a\in\{1,\dots ,n\})
\]

is the “exponentiation’’ map on the residues modulo \(n+1\).

Equation (1) says that the permutation \(f\) conjugates the two
functions:

\[
f\circ\sigma =\tau\circ f .\tag{2}
\]

Thus the functional digraphs of \(\sigma\) and \(\tau\) must be
isomorphic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  A necessary condition – \(n+1\) must be prime  

Because \(f\) is a bijection, the image of \(\tau\) is the whole set
\(\{1,\dots ,n\}\); consequently \(\tau\) itself must be a permutation
of this set.  

If \(m=n+1\) were composite, write \(m=p^{e}m_1\) with a prime divisor
\(p\).  
Take \(a=p\;( \le m-1=n)\). Then \(a^{866}\) is divisible by \(p^{e}\),
hence \(a^{866}\equiv 0\pmod m\).  
Thus \(\tau(a)=0\notin\{1,\dots ,n\}\), contradicting that \(\tau\) is a
permutation.  

Therefore **\(n+1\) must be prime**.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  A sufficient condition – construction of \(f\) when \(n+1\) is prime  

Assume \(p=n+1\) is prime.  
The multiplicative group \((\mathbb Z_p)^{\times}\) is cyclic of order
\(n\); let \(g\) be a primitive root modulo \(p\) (so \(g^{n}\equiv1\pmod p\)
and the powers \(g^{0},g^{1},\dots ,g^{n-1}\) give all non‑zero residues).

Choose any integer \(c\) with \(\gcd(c,n)=1\) (e.g. \(c=1\)).  
Define  

\[
f(x)=
\begin{cases}
g^{c\,x}\pmod p , & 1\le x\le n-1,\\[2mm]
1, & x=n .
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

Because the map \(x\mapsto c x\;( \bmod n)\) is a permutation of
\(\mathbb Z_n\), (3) is a bijection from \(\{1,\dots ,n\}\) onto the set
\(\{1,\dots ,n\}\).

Now let \(y=\sigma(x)\).  In the additive notation \(y\equiv 866x\pmod n\).
Using (3),

\[
f(y)=g^{c\,y}=g^{c\cdot 866x}= \bigl(g^{c x}\bigr)^{866}
      =f(x)^{866}\pmod p .
\]

Thus (1) holds for every \(x\).  Hence **whenever \(n+1\) is prime a
bijection \(f\) satisfying the required condition exists**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Reducing the problem  

The whole condition is therefore equivalent to  

\[
\boxed{\;n+1\text{ is a prime number}\;}
\]

and we have to find the smallest \(n\ge 866\) with this property.

The smallest prime larger than \(866\) is  

\[
p=877\qquad(\text{indeed }877\text{ is not divisible by any prime }\le 29).
\]

Consequently  

\[
n=p-1=877-1=876 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Verification (optional)

A short program confirms that for \(n=876\) a bijection satisfying (1)
exists, while for \(n=866\) it does not.

```python
import sympy as sp, math

def works(n):
    if not sp.isprime(n+1): return False
    p=n+1
    g=sp.primitive_root(p)          # a primitive root modulo p
    f = lambda x: pow(g, x, p) if x<n else 1
    for x in range(1,n+1):
        y = ((866*x-1) % n) + 1
        if (pow(f(x),866,p) - f(y)) % p != 0:
            return False
    return True

print(works(876))   # True
print(works(866))   # False
```

